Time: O(n * d * 2^d)
Space: O(logn)

Time complexity

A string with d digits has d - 1 possible cut positions. Each position can either:
contain a cut
not contain a cut
Therefore, there are up to:
2^d−1
possible partitions.
In this code:
num = int(square[start:end + 1])
creating the substring and converting it to an integer can take up to O(d) time.
For one integer i, the time is:
O(d⋅2^d)

We check all integers from 1 through n, so the total time is: O(n⋅d⋅2^d)
	​
Space complexity
The deepest recursive branch partitions every digit separately:
"1" | "2" | "9" | "6"
Therefore, the recursion depth is at most d:
O(d)
Since d=O(logn): O(logn)
	​

Final answer
Time: O(n⋅d⋅2^d)
Space: O(d) = O(logn)
	​



